---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import study from '../../lib/kinship-study.json'

// Aggregate results only. The underlying pedigrees belong to the Quintessa
// database (quintessa.net.au) and are not republished here: no dog
// names, ids or dog-level lists appear on this page.

const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US')
const pct = (n: number) => `${n.toFixed(0)}%`

const FAMILY: Record<string, { label: string; cls: string }> = {
  fawn: { label: 'Fawn / brindle', cls: 'f-fawn' },
  harl: { label: 'Harlequin / mantle', cls: 'f-harl' },
  blue: { label: 'Blue / black', cls: 'f-blue' },
  other: { label: 'Unassigned', cls: 'f-other' },
}

const at = (k: number) => study.curve[k - 1]
const years = Object.entries(study.by_year)
const registryLabel = (r: string) => (r === 'none' ? 'unregistered' : r.replace(' (US)', ''))
const colorLabel: Record<string, string> = {
  fawn: 'fawn',
  brindle: 'brindle',
  'harlequin/mantle/merle': 'harl./mantle/merle',
  blue: 'blue',
  black: 'black',
  unknown: 'colour unrecorded',
  other: 'other',
}
const topColors = (c: [string, number][], n: number) =>
  c
    .filter(([k]) => k !== 'unknown')
    .slice(0, 2)
    .map(([k, v]) => `${colorLabel[k] ?? k} ${Math.round((100 * v) / n)}%`)
    .join(', ')
const topRegs = (r: [string, number][], n: number) =>
  r
    .slice(0, 1)
    .map(([k, v]) => `${registryLabel(k)} ${Math.round((100 * v) / n)}%`)
    .join(', ')
const assigned = study.clusters.filter(c => c.cluster !== 0)
const within = assigned.map(c => c.within_kinship)
const between = assigned.map(c => c.to_rest_kinship)
const H = study.history
const coiPeak = H.coi.reduce((a, b) => (b.good > a.good ? b : a))
const coiAt = (y: number) => H.coi.find(r => r.y === y)!
const colAt = (y: number) => H.colour.find(r => r.y === y)!
const recAt = (y: number) => H.recording.find(r => r.y === y)!
const recMaxMerle = H.recording.filter(r => r.y <= 2005).reduce((a, b) => (b.merle > a.merle ? b : a))
const recMinMerle = H.recording.filter(r => r.y > recMaxMerle.y && r.y <= 2015).reduce((a, b) => (b.merle < a.merle ? b : a))
const winLabel = (y: number) => `${y - H.window / 2 + 1}–${y + H.window / 2}`
const cnt = (y: number) => (H.counts as Record<string, number>)[String(y)] ?? 0
const range = (a: number[]) => `${Math.min(...a).toFixed(2)}–${Math.max(...a).toFixed(2)}`
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Great Dane Kinship Study | 7Sisters Farm"
  canonical="https://7sistersgreatdanes.com/private/kinship-study"
>
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" slot="head" />
  <script is:inline src="/private-gate.js" data-key="kinship" data-codes="1887,1959" slot="head"></script>

  <article class="ks-root">
    <header class="ks-head">
      <p class="ks-kicker">Research draft · private</p>
      <h1>How related are Great Danes, and which few dogs would represent them?</h1>
      <p class="ks-lede">
        A pedigree kinship analysis of {fmt(study.reference)} Great Danes born
        2012–2020, using {fmt(study.dogs_loaded)} pedigree records from the
        Quintessa Great Dane database. The goal: cluster the breed by
        relatedness and pick a small set of dogs whose DNA sequencing would
        tell us the most about the whole population.
      </p>
      <p class="ks-byline">
        Dustin Mayfield-Jones, 7 Sisters Great Danes · pedigree source: the{' '}
        <a href="https://www.quintessa.net.au/" rel="noopener">Quintessa Great Dane database</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="ks-tiles" aria-label="Headline numbers">
      <div class="ks-tile">
        <span class="ks-tile-value">{study.mean_F.toFixed(3)}</span>
        <span class="ks-tile-label">average inbreeding (F), about a half-sib mating</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ks-tile">
        <span class="ks-tile-value">≈ {study.Ne}</span>
        <span class="ks-tile-label">effective population size</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ks-tile">
        <span class="ks-tile-value">{assigned.length}</span>
        <span class="ks-tile-label">lines, which follow colour families</span>
      </div>
      <div class="ks-tile">
        <span class="ks-tile-value">{pct(at(10).all)}</span>
        <span class="ks-tile-label">of dogs have a half-sib or closer relative among the top 10 sequencing picks</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Key findings</h2>
      <ol class="ks-findings">
        <li>
          <strong>The breed is several populations, not one.</strong> Kinship
          inside a cluster is {range(within)}, against {range(between)}{' '}
          between a cluster and everyone else, a 3–4× gap. The clusters line
          up with colour families: fawn/brindle, harlequin/mantle, and
          blue/black are largely bred as separate lines.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Inbreeding is high and flat.</strong> Mean F is{' '}
          {study.mean_F.toFixed(3)}, it sits between{' '}
          {Math.min(...years.map(([, v]) => v.F)).toFixed(3)} and{' '}
          {Math.max(...years.map(([, v]) => v.F)).toFixed(3)} every year from 2012 to 2020,
          and {study.frac_F_over_quarter}% of dogs are at or above 0.25 (the
          level of a full-sib or parent–offspring mating). Average kinship
          across the whole population is only {study.mean_kinship.toFixed(3)},
          so most of that inbreeding comes from breeding within lines.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A few dogs cover a lot, but not evenly.</strong> Two
          well-chosen dogs give {pct(at(2).all)} of the population a
          half-sib-or-closer relative in the sequenced set; forty reach{' '}
          {pct(at(40).all)}. The greedy picks favour the big fawn/brindle line:
          after ten picks, {pct(at(10).fawn)} of fawn/brindle dogs are covered
          but only {pct(at(10).blue)} of blue/black dogs.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>1. Population structure</h2>
      <p>
        Each dot is one dog, placed by the two strongest axes of the
        relationship matrix (principal coordinates; they explain{' '}
        {study.pca_var[0]}% and {study.pca_var[1]}% of the variation). Dogs
        close together share more ancestry. Colour is the cluster's colour
        family, found by clustering the kinship matrix, not from the coat
        colour field.
      </p>
      <figure class="ks-figure">
        <div class="ks-legend" id="pca-legend"></div>
        <div class="ks-selection" id="pca-selection" hidden>
          <span id="pca-selection-text"></span>
          <button type="button" id="pca-clear">Show all clusters</button>
        </div>
        <div class="ks-plot" id="pca-wrap">
          <canvas id="pca" role="img" aria-label="Scatter plot of dogs by the first two principal coordinates of pedigree relationship, coloured by colour-family cluster; the fawn/brindle, harlequin/mantle and blue/black groups occupy separate regions."></canvas>
          <div class="ks-tip" id="pca-tip" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Principal coordinates of the additive relationship matrix,{' '}
          {fmt(study.reference)} dogs born 2012–2020. Hover a dot to see its cluster; click a
          dot or a row in the table below to highlight that cluster.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ks-table-wrap">
        <table class="ks-table">
          <caption>Clusters from average-linkage clustering of pedigree kinship · click a row to highlight it in the plot</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Cluster</th>
              <th scope="col">Colour family</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Dogs</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Mean F</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Kinship within</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Kinship to rest</th>
              <th scope="col">Recorded colours</th>
              <th scope="col">Main registry</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              study.clusters
                .filter(c => c.cluster !== 0)
                .concat(study.clusters.filter(c => c.cluster === 0))
                .map(c => (
                  <tr class="ks-cluster-row" data-cluster={c.cluster}>
                    <td>
                      <button type="button" class="ks-row-btn" aria-pressed="false" aria-label={`Highlight ${c.cluster === 0 ? 'unassigned dogs' : `cluster ${c.cluster}`} in the plot`}>
                        {c.cluster === 0 ? '—' : c.cluster}
                      </button>
                    </td>
                    <td class="nowrap">
                      <span class={`ks-swatch ${FAMILY[c.family].cls}`} aria-hidden="true" />
                      {FAMILY[c.family].label}
                    </td>
                    <td class="num">{fmt(c.n)}</td>
                    <td class="num">{c.mean_F.toFixed(3)}</td>
                    <td class="num">{c.within_kinship.toFixed(3)}</td>
                    <td class="num">{c.to_rest_kinship.toFixed(3)}</td>
                    <td>{topColors(c.colors as [string, number][], c.n)}</td>
                    <td class="nowrap">{topRegs(c.registries as [string, number][], c.n)}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="ks-note">
        "Unassigned" is {fmt(study.clusters.find(c => c.cluster === 0)!.n)} dogs in
        groups smaller than 30. Most have shallow recorded pedigrees (about{' '}
        {study.clusters.find(c => c.cluster === 0)!.ecg} equivalent generations
        against about 25 for everyone else), so they look unrelated because
        their ancestry is missing, not because it is different.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>2. Inbreeding</h2>
      <p>
        F is the probability that a dog's two copies of a gene come from the
        same ancestor. Half of the dogs sit between{' '}
        {study.F_quantiles[1].toFixed(2)} and {study.F_quantiles[3].toFixed(2)}.
      </p>
      <figure class="ks-figure">
        <div class="ks-plot" id="hist-wrap">
          <svg id="hist" role="img" aria-label="Histogram of inbreeding coefficients. Most dogs fall between 0.075 and 0.175."></svg>
          <div class="ks-tip" id="hist-tip" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Number of dogs by inbreeding coefficient (bins of 0.025).</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="ks-table-wrap">
        <table class="ks-table ks-compact">
          <caption>By birth year</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Born</th>
              {years.map(([y]) => <th scope="col" class="num">{y}</th>)}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Dogs</th>
              {years.map(([, v]) => <td class="num">{fmt(v.n)}</td>)}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Mean F</th>
              {years.map(([, v]) => <td class="num">{v.F.toFixed(3)}</td>)}
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Pedigree depth</th>
              {years.map(([, v]) => <td class="num">{v.ecg.toFixed(1)}</td>)}
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="ks-note">
        Pedigree depth is equivalent complete generations: about 25 on
        average, which is very deep for a dog breed, so these estimates rest
        on well-documented ancestry. Effective population size (≈ {study.Ne})
        comes from each dog's individual rate of inbreeding per generation
        (Gutiérrez et al. 2008). Values under 100 are generally treated as a
        conservation concern.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>3. Choosing dogs to sequence</h2>
      <p>
        Dogs are added one at a time, each time taking the dog that most
        raises the average relationship between every dog in the population
        and its closest sequenced relative. A sequenced half-sib (relationship
        0.25) or closer is the benchmark for "covered", since that is roughly
        where a sequenced relative becomes useful for imputing another dog's
        genome from a cheap SNP panel. Candidates were limited to dogs born
        2016 or later.
      </p>
      <figure class="ks-figure">
        <div class="ks-legend" id="cov-legend"></div>
        <div class="ks-plot" id="cov-wrap">
          <svg id="cov" role="img" aria-label="Line chart of the share of dogs with a half-sib or closer sequenced relative as picks are added. Fawn/brindle coverage rises fastest, blue/black slowest."></svg>
          <div class="ks-tip" id="cov-tip" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Share of dogs with a half-sib-or-closer relative among the first{' '}
          <em>n</em> picks, overall and by colour family.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="ks-table-wrap">
        <table class="ks-table ks-compact">
          <caption>Coverage at selected pick counts</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Picks</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">All dogs</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Fawn / brindle</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Harlequin / mantle</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Blue / black</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Full-sib or closer</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              [1, 2, 3, 5, 10, 20, 40].map(k => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{k}</th>
                  <td class="num">{pct(at(k).all)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(at(k).fawn)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(at(k).harl)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(at(k).blue)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(at(k).full)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="ks-note">
        Of the 40 picks, {study.curve.filter(r => r.cluster === 1).length} come
        from the fawn/brindle main line and{' '}
        {study.curve.filter(r => r.cluster === 2).length} from the US
        harlequin/mantle line. The blue/black lines get their first pick at
        number {study.curve.findIndex(r => [4, 5, 6, 7, 11, 12].includes(r.cluster)) + 1}.
        A stratified design (a fixed budget per colour family) would cover the
        smaller lines far sooner. The individual picks are not listed here.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>4. Data quality</h2>
      <p>
        The pedigree is remarkably complete: about{' '}
        {pct((100 * 99500) / study.dogs_loaded)} of dogs have both parents on
        record. Before the analysis, {study.links_dropped} parent links that
        can't be correct (about 0.15% of all links) were set aside:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>{study.issues['parent born after child']} where the parent's recorded birth date is after the offspring's;</li>
        <li>{study.issues['bridged']} where climbing through undated ancestors leads to a dog born after its supposed descendant, often ids one or two apart, which suggests an entry slip;</li>
        <li>{study.issues['pedigree loop']} loops in which a dog would be its own ancestor.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Without these fixes the longest ancestral chain was 157 generations;
        with them it is {study.generations}, running in date order back to the
        early 1900s. The full list is available to the database maintainer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>5. Breeding choices through history</h2>
      <p>
        The same database reaches back to the 1880s, so we can ask how
        breeders' choices changed. Everything here uses {fmt(H.dated)} dogs
        with a recorded birth year and {fmt(H.litters)} litters, summarised
        over a rolling {H.window}-year window.
      </p>

      <h3>How many dogs, and from where</h3>
      <p>
        Registration numbers show which registry each dog came from: AKC
        numbers for the United States, state-prefixed and ANKC numbers for
        Australia, and stud books such as VDH, LOF, LOI and NHSB for
        continental Europe.
      </p>
      <figure class="ks-figure">
        <div class="ks-legend" id="region-legend"></div>
        <div class="ks-plot" id="count-wrap">
          <svg id="count" role="img" aria-label="Dogs recorded per birth year by registry region, 1880 to 2020. European dogs dominate until the Second World War; American dogs dominate from the late 1940s, with an Australian share from the 1970s. Totals dip in both world wars and peak in the late 1990s."></svg>
          <div class="ks-tip" id="count-tip" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Dogs in the database by birth year and registry region. Shaded: 1914–1918 and 1939–1945.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="ks-figure">
        <div class="ks-plot" id="share-wrap">
          <svg id="share" role="img" aria-label="Share of each birth cohort by registry region in five-year bins. Continental Europe falls from over 80 percent before 1930 to under 20 percent after 1960, as the United States rises to a majority."></svg>
          <div class="ks-tip" id="share-tip" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>The same dogs as shares of each five-year birth cohort.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The breed's centre of gravity crossed the Atlantic during the Second
        World War. Until the 1930s the record is almost entirely European,
        mostly German stud-book dogs. American dogs appear in the 1930s, and
        in 1945–49 they outnumber European dogs for the first time, more
        than doubling in five years while European numbers stayed flat.
        From then on the United States supplies most of the record,
        peaking at {fmt(cnt(1996))} dogs recorded for 1996 alone. Australia
        joins in the 1960s and holds a steady 10–16% share from the 1970s to
        the 2000s. Both wars cut total numbers too: from {cnt(1914)} dogs in
        1914 to {cnt(1917)} in 1917, and from {cnt(1938)} in 1938 to{' '}
        {cnt(1940)} in 1940, rebounding to {cnt(1947)} by 1947.
      </p>
      <div class="ks-table-wrap">
        <table class="ks-table ks-compact">
          <caption>Share of dogs by registry region and birth decade</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Born</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Dogs</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">United States</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Continental Europe</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Australia</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">Other</th>
              <th scope="col" class="num">No reg. no.</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              H.regions.map(r => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{r.decade}s</th>
                  <td class="num">{fmt(r.n)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(r['United States'])}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(r['Continental Europe'])}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(r.Australia)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(r.Other + r['United Kingdom'])}</td>
                  <td class="num">{pct(r.Unrecorded)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="ks-note">
        These are dogs in this database, not national registrations, so the
        counts also reflect how completely each era and country was entered.
        "Other" includes UK and Scandinavian numbers, which don't have a
        distinctive format. Because the shift happens right at the Second
        World War, the post-war inbreeding peak below reflects both wartime
        losses and a breed being rebuilt on a new continent.
      </p>

      <h3>How closely breeders inbred</h3>
      <p>
        Full-depth inbreeding climbs over time partly because older pedigrees
        are shorter. To compare eras fairly, this uses <strong>10-generation
        COI</strong>, which counts only ancestors within ten generations, and
        limits the main line to dogs whose ten generations are mostly known
        (at least 7 equivalent complete generations).
      </p>
      <figure class="ks-figure">
        <div class="ks-legend" id="coi-legend"></div>
        <div class="ks-plot" id="coi-wrap">
          <svg id="coi" role="img" aria-label="Mean 10-generation COI over time. It rises to a peak just after the Second World War and declines steadily from the 1950s to 2020."></svg>
          <div class="ks-tip" id="coi-tip" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Mean 10-generation COI, rolling {H.window}-year window.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="ks-figure">
        <div class="ks-plot" id="close-wrap">
          <svg id="close" role="img" aria-label="Share of well-documented dogs with 10-generation COI of 0.25 or more. It peaks near 13 percent after the Second World War and falls to about 1 percent by 2020."></svg>
          <div class="ks-tip" id="close-tip" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Share of well-documented dogs with 10-generation COI ≥ 0.25, the
          level of a full-sib or parent–offspring mating.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Close inbreeding peaked right after the Second World War: dogs born{' '}
        {winLabel(coiPeak.y)} averaged {coiPeak.good.toFixed(3)}, and{' '}
        {coiPeak.close.toFixed(0)}% were at or above 0.25, consistent with
        rebuilding the breed from the few dogs left after the war. Since then
        it has fallen steadily, to {coiAt(2020).good.toFixed(3)} with{' '}
        {coiAt(2020).close.toFixed(1)}% at 0.25 or above by the end of the
        record. Breeders now avoid close matings much more than they used to.
        Full-depth inbreeding is still about {study.mean_F.toFixed(2)},
        roughly double the 10-generation figure, so most of today's
        inbreeding is inherited from distant shared ancestors rather than
        created by recent matings.
      </p>

      <h3>Colour pairings</h3>
      <p>
        Breed clubs have long asked breeders to keep three colour families
        apart: fawn/brindle, harlequin/mantle (with black), and blue (with
        black). The documented milestones:
      </p>
      <ol class="ks-timeline">
        <li><strong>1880–1891.</strong> Germany fixes the name Deutsche Dogge (1880), founds the Deutscher Doggen-Club (1888) and issues the first standard (1891). The club notes that early breeders were "much more liberal" about separating colours than later ones.</li>
        <li><strong>1954–1959.</strong> Lina Basquette shows CH Honey Hollow Stormi Rudio, a fawn from a fawn × black mating. He is the No. 1 Working dog in the US from 1957 to 1959 and wins the 1959 Westminster Working Group. The backlash is described as the start of the US "colour code wars".</li>
        <li><strong>GDCA colour code.</strong> Part of the GDCA Code of Ethics for decades (the adoption year isn't published online). Blue may not be bred to fawn/brindle, or to harlequin or black-from-harlequin; fawn/brindle may not be bred into harlequin lines; and dogs had to be clear of other colours for four generations. It does not forbid black × fawn, and black has long been crossed with fawn and brindle to improve blacks.</li>
        <li><strong>UK Breed Council colour code.</strong> Its list of approved matings pairs black only with blue, black, harlequin and mantle; fawn/brindle × harlequin/mantle is not recommended. Under revision since 2021.</li>
        <li><strong>1988.</strong> Neil O'Sullivan and Phillip Sponenberg publish evidence in <em>Genetica</em> that harlequin is a modification of merle. DNA work at Texas A&amp;M and Clemson later finds both genes.</li>
        <li><strong>2000.</strong> The GDCA approves black mantle as a colour.</li>
        <li><strong>2012.</strong> The FCI (European) standard drops grey merle as a disqualifying fault.</li>
        <li><strong>2015.</strong> GDCA members vote by a large margin to accept merle.</li>
        <li><strong>2019.</strong> The AKC recognises merle on 1 January, and the first merle champion finishes that April. The GDCA takes its colour code down for a revision built around DNA colour testing.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Using the recorded colour of both parents
        ({fmt(H.litters_with_colour)} litters), each litter is classed as within
        a family (fawn × fawn/brindle, harlequin × harlequin/mantle,
        blue × blue, and black with harlequin, blue or black) or as a
        cross between families. The dashed lines mark 1959, 2000, 2015 and 2019.
      </p>
      <figure class="ks-figure">
        <div class="ks-plot" id="cross-wrap">
          <svg id="cross" role="img" aria-label="Share of litters from cross-family colour pairings over time."></svg>
          <div class="ks-tip" id="cross-tip" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>All cross-family pairings, share of litters with both parents' colours recorded.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="ks-multiples" id="pairs"></div>
      <p>
        Early breeders crossed freely: {colAt(1895).blue_fawn.toFixed(0)}% of
        litters around {winLabel(1895)} were blue × fawn. Those crosses had
        almost disappeared by the 1930s, and fawn × harlequin and blue ×
        fawn have stayed around 1% ever since. The exception is{' '}
        <strong>black × fawn</strong>, which has grown from about{' '}
        {colAt(1945).black_fawn.toFixed(0)}% of litters after the war to{' '}
        {colAt(2015).black_fawn.toFixed(0)}% around {winLabel(2015)}. That
        growth is why cross-family litters overall went from{' '}
        {colAt(2000).cross.toFixed(0)}% around {winLabel(2000)} to{' '}
        {colAt(2020).cross.toFixed(0)}% in the last years of the record.
      </p>
      <p>
        That fits the codes. The crosses the GDCA code forbids outright
        (blue × fawn, blue × harlequin, fawn × harlequin) stay rare. Black ×
        fawn, the pairing the US code leaves open and the UK code leaves off
        its approved list, is the one that grew. It more than doubled through
        the 1950s, the decade of the Stormi Rudio controversy, held near 2%
        for forty years, then climbed again from the mid-2000s.
      </p>
      <p class="ks-note">
        Colour is recorded for about half of dogs, and a litter counts only
        when both parents have a colour. Black is shared by the harlequin and
        blue families, so black × harlequin and black × blue count as within
        family.
      </p>

      <h3>Recognition changed the records</h3>
      <p>
        When a colour is recognised, breeders record it by name. Among
        harlequin-family dogs (harlequin, mantle, merle and white, not
        counting solid blacks), the share recorded as mantle was already
        rising before the GDCA approved it in 2000, and it kept climbing
        after: {pct(recAt(1995).mantle)} around {winLabel(1995)},{' '}
        {pct(recAt(2005).mantle)} around {winLabel(2005)} and{' '}
        {pct(recAt(2015).mantle)} around {winLabel(2015)}. Merle shows up in
        the record even while it was a disqualifying fault, peaking near{' '}
        {pct(recMaxMerle.merle)} around {winLabel(recMaxMerle.y)}. It then
        fell to {pct(recMinMerle.merle)} around {winLabel(recMinMerle.y)}{' '}
        and rose again to {pct(recAt(2020).merle)} after the 2015 vote,
        although the last years hold fewer dogs.
      </p>
      <figure class="ks-figure">
        <div class="ks-legend" id="rec-legend"></div>
        <div class="ks-plot" id="rec-wrap">
          <svg id="rec" role="img" aria-label="Share of harlequin-family dogs recorded as mantle and as merle, 1950 to 2020. Mantle rises steadily from under 10 percent to over 30 percent; merle rises to about 10 percent in the 1990s, dips in the late 2000s and rises again after 2015."></svg>
          <div class="ks-tip" id="rec-tip" hidden></div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Share of harlequin-family dogs recorded as mantle or merle, rolling{' '}
          {H.window}-year window. Dashed lines: 2000 (mantle approved), 2015
          (GDCA vote on merle), 2019 (AKC accepts merle).
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="ks-note">
        This matters for reading the pairings above. Some dogs once recorded
        as black are now recorded as mantle, so part of the fall in
        black × harlequin litters in the 2010s is a change in labels, not
        in breeding.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>6. What sequencing could tell us</h2>
      <p>
        The pedigree records who bred to whom for 140 years; genomes record
        what those choices did. Great Danes are unusual in having both a deep,
        dated pedigree and a documented set of breeding rules (the colour
        codes), which makes the breed a natural experiment in how human
        decisions reshape a genome. Below are the questions we find most
        interesting, ranked by the science rather than by cost, with the
        figures each paper would be built around. Novelty is judged against a
        literature search in September 2026 and would need a fuller review
        before publication.
      </p>

      <h3>What has already been done</h3>
      <ul class="ks-prior">
        <li><strong>German Shepherds, 120 years of genomes (PNAS 2025).</strong> Nine museum specimens (1906–1993) against modern dogs: heterozygosity fell about 25%, runs of homozygosity roughly doubled (from about 19% to 36% of the genome), bottlenecks line up with popular sires and WWII, and harmful-variant load rose. The study had no pedigree and no health outcomes, and its authors call for time series from other breeds.</li>
        <li><strong>New mutations across 43 breeds (Genome Biology 2025).</strong> 390 parent–offspring trios: larger breeds carry more mutations arising early in development, and fathers' age matters 1.5× more than in humans.</li>
        <li><strong>Balto (Science 2023).</strong> One historic sled dog's genome captured diversity since lost from modern breeds.</li>
        <li><strong>Great Danes specifically:</strong> one long-read reference genome (Zoey, 2021); 83 low-pass genomes for a megaesophagus study (2023); immune-gene variants linked to bloat (2017); population structure only from small marker panels (10 microsatellites in 88 dogs, which separated American from European Danes; 33 markers in 43 dogs at UC Davis); giant size partly mapped to loci on the X chromosome in a study of 19 giant breeds; merle and harlequin genes known down to merle insertion length.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Two gaps stand out. Nobody has looked at Great Dane colour lines
        genome-wide, and no historical genome series, in any breed, has had a
        full pedigree to test against.
      </p>

      <h3>Why Great Danes? A comparison with the German Shepherd study</h3>
      <p>
        Great Danes aren't a better population across the board. They are
        better for some questions and worse for others, and the strongest
        case is as a contrast to German Shepherds, not a replacement.
      </p>
      <div class="ks-compare">
        <div class="ks-compare-col">
          <p class="ks-compare-head">Where Great Danes are stronger</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The rules target known genes.</strong> The colour codes decided which matings to avoid, and those decisions act on genes we can read directly: merle, harlequin, blue, and the fawn/black switch. A rule's effect can be measured twice: at the genes it aimed at, and across the whole genome as a side effect. German Shepherd breeding is also tightly regulated (the German club's breed surveys, and the split into working and show lines), but those rules select on looks and performance, which are spread across many genes.</li>
            <li><strong>Several populations within one breed.</strong> This analysis finds {assigned.length} lines following the colour families, with 3–4× more kinship within lines than between them. That allows comparisons inside the breed: the same era, different rules. The German Shepherd study followed essentially one population through time.</li>
            <li><strong>A deep, dated pedigree.</strong> The German Shepherd study had nine museum genomes and no pedigree; it inferred bottlenecks from the DNA and linked them to popular sires afterwards. Here, 140 years of dated pedigree let every genomic estimate be checked against a known history.</li>
            <li><strong>Two dated events to recover.</strong> WWII, and the move of the breed's centre from Germany to America (American dogs first outnumber European ones in 1945–49), are fixed dates the genome should be able to find.</li>
            <li><strong>An extreme body.</strong> Giant size and a short life make Danes a natural test of the finding that bigger breeds gain more new mutations early in development. A mid-sized breed can't address that.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ks-compare-col">
          <p class="ks-compare-head">Where German Shepherds are stronger</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Specimens.</strong> One of the world's most common breeds, with a deep museum collection in Bern. Whether dated historical Great Danes survive in museums is still unknown, and that is the biggest risk to question 2 below.</li>
            <li><strong>A clean start.</strong> The breed was founded in 1899 around a single dog, Horand von Grafrath. Great Danes merged several regional types around 1880, so there is no single founding moment to anchor the story.</li>
            <li><strong>Scale and health data.</strong> Far more dogs to sample, and much more health data (hips, degenerative myelopathy), which matters for questions about genetic load.</li>
            <li><strong>Wolf ancestry.</strong> Documented wolf crosses let that study watch admixture fade under closed breeding. Great Danes have no equivalent.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>The pitch:</strong> two breeds formed in the same country in
        the same decades and hit by the same war, but bred under different
        rules. German Shepherds are one population selected for work; Great
        Danes were divided by colour codes and then moved to a new continent.
        Running the same methods on both makes each result mean more, and it
        answers the German Shepherd authors' own call for time series from
        breeds with different histories.
      </p>

      <h3>The questions, most interesting first</h3>
      <div class="ks-papers">
        <article class="ks-paper">
          <p class="ks-paper-rank">1 · start here</p>
          <h4>Can a breed club's rulebook split a population? Colour codes as a natural experiment</h4>
          <p><strong>Question.</strong> For decades, breeders kept fawn/brindle, harlequin/mantle and blue/black apart by agreement, not geography. Did that written rule create genetically distinct subpopulations, as a river or a mountain range would? Did it work at the colour genes it targeted: do fawn lines still hide blue alleles, and solid lines cryptic merle? And does the growing black × fawn crossing show up as gene flow?</p>
          <p><strong>Figures.</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Genomic PCA beside the pedigree PCA in section 1, for the same dogs, aligned so each dog can be compared.</li>
            <li>Genetic differentiation (F<sub>ST</sub>) between the {assigned.length} lines, set against how many generations the pedigree says they have been apart.</li>
            <li>Colour-gene allele frequencies by line, including the length spectrum of merle insertions.</li>
            <li>Ancestry bars with black × fawn offspring highlighted.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Why first.</strong> It is the question only Great Danes can answer cleanly: several colour families under written rules with a documented history, including exactly when the rules bent. It asks something general, how cultural rules shape genomes. And it defines the lines, the unit every later question is measured on. It needs only living dogs.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="ks-paper">
          <p class="ks-paper-rank">2 · the flagship</p>
          <h4>140 years of Great Dane genomes, read against the pedigree that made them</h4>
          <p><strong>Question.</strong> Sequence historical Great Danes (museum skeletons, teeth, taxidermy and dated hair from about 1880 onward) alongside modern dogs. Did diversity fall the way the pedigree predicts? When did the colour lines actually diverge: before the codes, or because of them? What did WWII and the move from Germany to America do to the genome? Which popular sires left the deepest marks?</p>
          <p><strong>Figures.</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>The headline: heterozygosity and runs of homozygosity by birth year, with each historical genome plotted against the pedigree's prediction for that same year.</li>
            <li>Historical dogs placed on the modern genomic map, showing the path from German to American Danes and the colour lines branching apart.</li>
            <li>Bottleneck dating from the genome, checked against the war years and popular sires named in the pedigree.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Why it could be a first.</strong> It would be the first historical genome series for Great Danes, and the first in any breed tested against a full, dated pedigree. That pedigree is exactly what the German Shepherd study lacked. Natural history museums hold breed collections (the German Shepherd specimens came from Bern), so finding dated Great Danes is the first practical step, and worth starting now.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="ks-paper">
          <p class="ks-paper-rank">3</p>
          <h4>Ground truth for genomic dating: a century of pedigree meets the genome</h4>
          <p><strong>Question.</strong> Geneticists date inbreeding from the lengths of runs of homozygosity: long runs mean recent inbreeding, short runs mean old. That method is rarely checked against a known history. Here we know the history: the post-WWII peak, the fall in close matings since, and which matings were close. How well does the genome recover it, and how well does pedigree relatedness predict real shared DNA?</p>
          <p><strong>Figures.</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Pedigree F against genomic inbreeding (F<sub>ROH</sub>) for each dog.</li>
            <li>Runs of homozygosity split by length, by birth cohort, against the pedigree's timeline.</li>
            <li>Realised against expected relatedness for relatives of each degree.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> It checks a method used across conservation and livestock genetics, using a population whose history is written down. It comes from the same genomes as questions 1 and 2.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="ks-paper">
          <p class="ks-paper-rank">4</p>
          <h4>Built big, aging fast: mutation in a giant breed</h4>
          <p><strong>Question.</strong> Great Danes are among the largest and shortest-lived dogs. The 2025 trio study found larger breeds gain more new mutations early in development. Does the giant extreme push this further? The pedigree identifies parent–offspring trios and parents' ages at mating from birth dates; lifespans aren't in the database and would have to come from owners.</p>
          <p><strong>Figures.</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>New mutations per offspring against sire and dam age, for Great Danes over the published curve for 43 breeds.</li>
            <li>The share of mutations arising early in development, by breed size.</li>
            <li>Size-locus genotypes (IGF1 and the X-chromosome giant loci) across the lines.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> It connects the breed's defining trait, size, to its hardest problem, a short life, at the level of the germline.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="ks-paper">
          <p class="ks-paper-rank">5</p>
          <h4>Did the bottleneck purge or concentrate harmful variants, line by line?</h4>
          <p><strong>Question.</strong> Every recorded case of inherited myopathy of Great Danes (IMGD) was fawn or brindle, a hint that colour-line structure also decides where disease alleles sit. Did the post-war bottleneck purge harmful variants or pile them up, and does that differ between lines?</p>
          <p><strong>Figures.</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Homozygous harmful variants per dog, by line and birth year, historical dogs included.</li>
            <li>Known disease alleles (IMGD, the bloat-associated immune alleles and others) by line.</li>
          </ul>
          <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> It would link genetic load to the breed's history and structure, the connection the German Shepherd study said it could not make. It also gives breeders a line-aware picture of risk.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="ks-paper">
          <p class="ks-paper-rank">6</p>
          <h4>Choosing which dogs to sequence</h4>
          <p><strong>Question.</strong> Does a reference panel chosen from the pedigree (section 3), with a set share per colour family, impute the breed better than one chosen at random or by convenience?</p>
          <p><strong>Figures.</strong> Imputation accuracy against panel size, for pedigree-selected, stratified and random panels, by colour family.</p>
          <p><strong>Why it matters.</strong> Standard in cattle, untested in a dog breed. It is a methods result more than a discovery, which is why it sits here, but it makes every later study cheaper.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="ks-paper">
          <p class="ks-paper-rank">7 · longer term</p>
          <h4>A Great Dane pangenome and health mapping</h4>
          <p><strong>Question.</strong> What large structural variants does each line carry beyond the single reference genome? With health records, which variants drive bloat, dilated cardiomyopathy, wobbler syndrome and bone cancer?</p>
          <p><strong>Figures.</strong> A pangenome graph with line-specific structural variants; association plots for each health trait.</p>
          <p><strong>Why later.</strong> It needs health outcomes on hundreds of dogs, best gathered with an established group, and it is far stronger once the lines from question 1 are known.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h3>Why this order</h3>
      <ol class="ks-why">
        <li><strong>Question 1 comes first because everything else is measured in its units.</strong> Whether the colour lines are real genetic populations decides how questions 2–7 are sampled and read. It is also the question most specific to Great Danes, and it has a general answer: whether rules alone can split a population.</li>
        <li><strong>Question 2 is the most exciting, and should start in parallel.</strong> It reuses question 1's modern genomes as its present-day end. The slow part is finding dated historical specimens, so that search should begin now.</li>
        <li><strong>Question 3 comes almost free with 1 and 2</strong> and tests a widely used method against written history.</li>
        <li><strong>Questions 4 and 5 turn the history into biology:</strong> what size and bottlenecks did to mutation and genetic load. They are richer once the lines and the time series exist.</li>
        <li><strong>Questions 6 and 7 are the most practical and the most crowded.</strong> They matter, but they are less distinctive science, and they lean on everything before them.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Limitations and next steps</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The data effectively stop in 2020.</strong> Dogs born
          2012–2020 stand in for the living population, so many picks are no
          longer available to sample. An updated database would move the
          analysis to living dogs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pedigree kinship is an expectation.</strong> Real shared DNA
          varies around it, which is exactly what sequencing measures;
          genotyped dogs would let us check these estimates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Colour family is inferred.</strong> Clusters were found from
          relatedness alone; the colour labels describe what most dogs in
          each cluster are recorded as. About a fifth of dogs have no colour
          on record.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Next:</strong> add post-2020 litters, rerun with a
          per-colour-family sequencing budget, and sequence living dogs
          across the lines for question 1 while searching museum collections
          for dated historical Great Danes (section 6).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

