    <details class="ks-methods">
      <summary>Methods</summary>
      <p>
        Pedigree records were parsed from a Quintessa database export and
        cleaned as described above. The pedigree was trimmed to the{' '}
        {fmt(study.trimmed)} ancestors of the reference population
        ({fmt(study.reference)} dogs born 2012–2020 with both parents
        recorded). Inbreeding coefficients were computed generation by
        generation as half the relationship between each dog's parents, using
        the decomposition A = TDT′ (T = (I − P)⁻¹, D the Mendelian sampling
        variances), with sparse triangular solves so the full matrix is never
        formed. The {fmt(study.reference)} × {fmt(study.reference)}{' '}
        relationship matrix was then computed the same way.
      </p>
      <p>
        Clustering: average-linkage hierarchical clustering on 1 − normalised
        relationship, cut to give twelve clusters of at least 30 dogs.
        Sequencing picks: greedy maximisation of the mean, over the reference
        population, of each dog's relationship to its closest selected dog.
        Effective population size: from individual increase in inbreeding,
        ΔFᵢ = 1 − (1 − Fᵢ)^(1/(tᵢ − 1)), with tᵢ the equivalent complete
        generations. Methods were checked against textbook cases (full-sib and
        parent–offspring matings).
      </p>
      <p>
        History: 10-generation COI for every dog with a birth year, computed
        with the tabular method on each dog's own pedigree cut at ten
        generations (ancestors further back by their shortest path are treated
        as unrelated founders). It is never higher than the full-depth value
        and correlates with it at 0.95 for 2012–2020 dogs. Litters are unique
        sire–dam–birth-year combinations. Rolling windows are ten birth years
        wide and shown only where they hold at least 50 well-documented dogs
        (COI) or 60 litters with both parents' colours (pairings).
      </p>
    </details>
